    fullDescription: "Founded by British colonists in 1859, the Singapore Botanic Gardens became the nation's first and only UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2015 — a distinction only a handful of tropical gardens anywhere have achieved. Its 82 hectares stretch from the busy edge of Orchard Road into a genuine patch of primary rainforest, threaded with lakes, lawns and heritage trees. Highlights include Swan Lake and Symphony Lake, the latter hosting free weekend concerts on its island stage; the Heritage Tree Trail, with more than 80 towering protected giants; the Healing Garden of medicinal plants; and Jacob Ballas Children's Garden, the first of its kind in Asia. The undoubted jewel is the National Orchid Garden (S$15), a separately ticketed hillside displaying more than 1,000 orchid species and 2,000 hybrids — the largest such collection on earth, and the birthplace of Vanda Miss Joaquim, Singapore's national flower. The gardens are also woven into daily life: at dawn thousands of Singaporeans jog and practise tai chi along the paths, and at dusk families spread out picnic blankets. The main gardens are free and open from 05:00 to midnight, making them a cool, green escape from the city's glass and concrete. Allow two to three hours for an unhurried loop, wear light clothing and mosquito repellent for the rainforest section, and bring water — it is a large and humid place.",
