    fullDescription: "Jewel Changi Airport is proof that even a transit hub can be a destination in its own right. Opened in 2019 and designed by Moshe Safdie, this vast steel-and-glass toroidal dome sits between the terminals of the airport routinely voted the world's best, and at its heart plunges the HSBC Rain Vortex — at 40 metres, the tallest indoor waterfall on the planet, fed by recycled rainwater and ringed by a lush terraced forest valley of 900 trees and 60,000 shrubs. It is free to admire from any of the surrounding levels, and after dark it becomes a light-and-sound spectacle, illuminated in shifting colours and set to music on the half-hour from 19:30. Around the waterfall spiral more than 280 shops and restaurants, but the real draw for families is the rooftop Canopy Park (from S$8), with its bouncing nets suspended high above the atrium, a hedge maze, a mirror maze, sculptural topiary and glass-bottomed Canopy Bridge. Because it is linked directly to the terminals and to the city by MRT, Jewel makes a brilliant way to fill a long layover or a rainy afternoon, and many travellers deliberately arrive early to enjoy it before a flight. Even if you are not flying, it is an easy 15-minute ride from the centre and an astonishing feat of engineering and greenery.",
