    fullDescription: "For many visitors, a plate of chilli crab or black pepper crab is the single most memorable meal in Singapore, and Long Beach Seafood is one of the best-loved places to have it. Founded in 1946, this veteran restaurant group even claims to have invented black pepper crab back in the 1950s — a mountain of wok-fried mud crab coated in a fiercely aromatic, buttery black-pepper sauce that many locals rate above the more famous chilli version. Both are here in their glory: the chilli crab arrives in a sweet, savoury, subtly spicy tomato-and-egg gravy that demands a basket of golden fried mantou buns for mopping. Beyond crab, the sprawling menu covers the full canon of Singaporean-Chinese seafood — cereal prawns, salted-egg-yolk crab, drunken prawns, steamed fish, sambal kang kong and more — served in the bustling, no-frills style of a proper local seafood house. The atmosphere is boisterous and family-friendly, with big round tables of locals and tourists alike cracking shells and passing dishes. The flagship at the East Coast Seafood Centre lets you dine within sight and sound of the sea, the classic setting for this kind of meal. Crab is priced by weight, so a feast for two typically lands around S$70 to S$110 plus sides. Book ahead for weekend dinners, wear the bib they offer, and come hungry and ready to get your hands messy — this is Singaporean eating at its most joyful.",
