    fullDescription: "Maxwell Food Centre is one of Singapore's most famous and accessible hawker centres, a covered hall of around a hundred stalls on the edge of Chinatown that offers a perfect introduction to the city's street-food culture — recognised by UNESCO in 2020 as an intangible cultural heritage. Its most celebrated tenant is Tian Tian Hainanese Chicken Rice, whose plates of tender poached chicken over fragrant, chicken-fat-scented rice, served with chilli and ginger sauces, are so revered that the late Anthony Bourdain sang their praises and the queue rarely lets up; a plate costs around S$5. But part of the joy of a hawker centre is grazing widely, so once you have your chicken rice, wander the aisles and pick up a plate of smoky char kway teow (stir-fried flat noodles), a fresh popiah (soft spring roll), a bowl of laksa, some silky chwee kueh (steamed rice cakes with preserved radish) and a glass of freshly pressed sugar-cane juice — most dishes cost only S$4 to S$8. Seating is communal and finding a table at peak lunchtime can be a friendly scramble; the local custom of 'choping' (reserving) a seat with a packet of tissues is very much in force. Cash is king at most stalls, though many now take cashless payment. Right beside the Buddha Tooth Relic Temple and Sri Mariamman Temple, Maxwell is easy to fold into a Chinatown afternoon and remains one of the best-value, most authentic meals in the whole city.",
