    fullDescription: "Tiong Bahru Market sits at the centre of one of Singapore's most charming and characterful neighbourhoods, and it offers a shopping and eating experience quite different from the malls and tourist markets. Tiong Bahru is the city's oldest public housing estate, a low-rise enclave of curved, streamlined 1930s Art Deco walk-up apartments that has in recent years become one of the trendiest quarters in the city, its heritage buildings now home to indie bookshops, design boutiques, bakeries and some of the best third-wave cafés in Singapore. The market itself is a wonderful slice of everyday local life: the ground floor is a traditional wet market where residents shop for fresh fish, meat, vegetables, fruit and flowers among the morning bustle, while the upper floor houses a beloved hawker centre renowned among Singaporeans for its breakfast and lunch stalls. Foodies make pilgrimages here for classics like chwee kueh (steamed rice cakes with preserved radish), lor mee, hokkien mee, curry puffs and traditional coffee, most dishes costing only S$4 to S$8. The best plan is to arrive in the morning to catch the market at its liveliest and eat a hawker breakfast, then spend an hour or two wandering the surrounding streets, browsing the independent shops and cafés and admiring the distinctive architecture. It is a favourite of design-minded locals and in-the-know visitors seeking a more authentic, neighbourhood side of Singapore away from the crowds. For a relaxed morning that combines a genuine local market, superb cheap food and boutique browsing in a uniquely atmospheric setting, Tiong Bahru is hard to beat.",
