    fullDescription: "The Mass Rapid Transit, universally known as the MRT, is the backbone of getting around Singapore and consistently rated among the finest metro systems in the world — clean, safe, punctual, air-conditioned and remarkably cheap. Its six colour-coded lines fan out across the island and, crucially for visitors, reach almost every place you are likely to want to go: Orchard Road, Marina Bay, Chinatown, Little India, Kampong Glam, Sentosa (via HarbourFront), the airport and far beyond. Trains are frequent, arriving every few minutes, and the stations are immaculate, well signed in English and often connected directly into malls and attractions via underground and covered links, a genuine blessing in the tropical heat and sudden downpours. Fares are calculated by distance and typically run from around S$1 to S$2.50 a trip, making it by far the most economical way to travel. Payment is refreshingly simple: you can tap a contactless credit or debit card straight onto the fare gates, use a stored-value EZ-Link card, or buy a Singapore Tourist Pass for unlimited daily travel if you plan a lot of hopping around. A few practical notes: eating, drinking and, famously, carrying durians are prohibited on board and can incur fines, so respect the rules; trains run from around 05:30 to midnight, so plan late nights accordingly; and rush hours can be crowded. Navigation apps like Citymapper or Google Maps make route-planning effortless. For the vast majority of visitors, the MRT combined with a little walking is all you need to explore Singapore comfortably and affordably, and using it is a pleasure rather than a chore.",
