    fullDescription: "Singapore's public bus network is comprehensive, inexpensive and a useful complement to the MRT, reaching residential neighbourhoods, parks and pockets of the island that the trains do not, and offering the bonus of a scenic, at-ground-level view of the city as you travel, especially from the top deck of a double-decker. The buses are modern, air-conditioned and clean, and the system is fully integrated with the MRT for payment: you simply tap the same contactless bank card, EZ-Link stored-value card or Singapore Tourist Pass on the reader when you board and again when you alight, with fares calculated by distance and typically costing between S$1 and S$2.30 — remembering to tap out is important to avoid being charged the maximum fare. The EZ-Link card, available from MRT station machines and ticket offices for a small cost plus stored value, is a handy option for those who prefer not to use a foreign bank card, and it works across all buses and trains; alternatively the Singapore Tourist Pass offers unlimited travel for one, two or three days if you intend to move around a lot. The main practical challenge for visitors is simply knowing which bus to catch, but this is easily solved with apps like Google Maps, Citymapper or the local bus-arrival apps, which give real-time arrival information and step-by-step routing. Services run from early morning until around midnight, with limited night-bus routes on Friday and Saturday nights. While most visitors will rely primarily on the MRT for its speed and simplicity, keeping the bus network in mind opens up cheaper, more direct journeys to certain attractions and a more local way of seeing the city.",
