    fullDescription: "Taxis and ride-hailing are a comfortable, affordable and stress-free way to get around Singapore, particularly for late-night journeys, trips with luggage, travel in a sudden tropical downpour, or reaching spots off the MRT network. Singapore's taxis are famously honest and well regulated: they are metered, drivers take the direct route, and there is no need to haggle or worry about being overcharged. Fares start with a flagfall of around S$3.90 to S$4.80 and build by distance and time, with various surcharges for peak hours, late nights, public holidays, airport trips and city-centre zones, but a typical hop across town still costs only around S$8 to S$15, very reasonable by the standards of most major cities. You can hail taxis on the street, pick them up at the ubiquitous ranks outside malls, hotels and MRT stations, or, most conveniently, book them through an app. Grab is the dominant ride-hailing platform in Singapore and the region — the local equivalent of Uber — and it is well worth downloading: it shows the fare upfront before you book, removing any uncertainty, lets you pay by linked card, and offers everything from budget cars to larger vehicles and even taxi bookings. Gojek and the taxi company ComfortDelGro's own app are alternatives. Grab prices are broadly comparable to metered taxis, sometimes cheaper off-peak and higher during surge periods. Between the two, you are never far from an easy ride. For most visitors, the ideal approach is to use the cheap and efficient MRT for the bulk of daytime sightseeing and turn to a taxi or Grab for convenience when it counts — arriving at a restaurant, heading home late, or beating the heat and rain.",
