    fullDescription: "The Singapore Tourist Pass is a special transit card offering unlimited travel on the MRT and public buses for one, two or three consecutive days, aimed at visitors who intend to move around the island heavily by public transport. Priced at roughly S$22 for one day, S$29 for two days and S$34 for three days (each including a small refundable card deposit reclaimable within a few days of purchase), it removes any need to think about individual fares and lets you hop on and off trains and buses as often as you like. Whether it actually saves you money, however, depends entirely on how much you travel: because Singapore's ordinary distance-based fares are already very cheap, at roughly S$1 to S$2.50 a trip, you need to make a good number of journeys each day — generally four or more longer trips — for the pass to pay for itself against simply tapping a contactless bank card or a pay-as-you-go EZ-Link card. For visitors on a packed, transport-heavy itinerary, criss-crossing the island between far-flung attractions like the Mandai zoos, the airport, Sentosa and downtown, the pass offers convenience and peace of mind and can work out cheaper. For those doing more relaxed, walkable sightseeing concentrated in the central districts, or who prefer to mix in taxis and Grab, pay-as-you-go is usually simpler and often cheaper. It can be bought at TransitLink ticket offices in major MRT stations, including at Changi Airport on arrival, and there is also a digital version. Do the rough maths against your plans before buying: for many light-to-moderate users, the humble tap of a contactless card is the easiest and most economical choice, but for the transport-intensive it is a handy, hassle-free option.",
