    fullDescription: "Singapore is a long-haul destination for most travellers, so flight prices form the biggest single cost of a trip, and timing your booking well can make a substantial difference. As a rough guide, round-trip economy fares to Changi tend to start from around $650 from Europe or Australia in the cheaper periods and climb to $800, $1,000 or more during the peak seasons, with prices from North America generally higher again given the distance. The cheapest month to fly is typically February, just after the Chinese New Year rush and outside the school holidays, along with the quieter shoulder periods of the year. The most expensive times to travel are the June-to-August northern summer, the December Christmas and New Year period, and around major regional holidays such as Chinese New Year itself, when both demand and prices surge. Because Changi is one of the world's great aviation hubs, served by a huge number of airlines and with excellent competition on many routes, there are frequently good deals to be found for those who are flexible. The classic advice holds: for long-haul fares, book roughly two to four months in advance rather than at the last minute or too far ahead, be flexible with your dates, and consider flying midweek, as Tuesday and Wednesday departures are often cheaper than weekend ones. Setting up fare alerts and comparing across airlines and dates, as our search tool lets you do, is the surest way to catch a bargain. Also weigh up whether a one-stop routing on a well-priced carrier undercuts a nonstop flight significantly. On the ground, Singapore is not a cheap city, so saving on the airfare leaves more of your budget for its superb food, attractions and hotels.",
