    fullDescription: "Singapore has a reputation as an expensive city, and for accommodation, alcohol and taxis it certainly can be, but it is also a place where careful travellers can eat superbly and get around cheaply, so your daily budget depends heavily on your choices. At the budget end, it is genuinely possible to enjoy Singapore on around S$80 to S$120 a day excluding your flight and hotel: hawker centres serve delicious, satisfying meals for just S$5 to S$8, the MRT and buses cost only a dollar or two a trip, and many of the city's best experiences — the Gardens by the Bay outdoor gardens and light show, the Botanic Gardens, the neighbourhoods of Chinatown, Little India and Kampong Glam, the Marina Bay waterfront and its nightly Spectra show, and the beaches — are entirely free. A comfortable mid-range budget of roughly S$200 to S$350 a day adds a mix of sit-down restaurant meals, paid attractions like the aquarium or a Sentosa theme park, the odd cocktail and taxi, and a decent three- or four-star hotel. At the luxury end, with five-star hotels, fine dining, rooftop bars and premium attractions, you can comfortably spend S$500 a day and far more. The biggest budget-busters are alcohol, which is heavily taxed (a beer easily costs S$12 to S$15 and a cocktail S$22 to S$30), branded shopping, and high-end hotels, whereas food, public transport and many attractions offer real value. Tipping is not customary or expected, as a 10% service charge and 9% GST are added to most restaurant bills. The smart approach is to eat like a local at hawker centres, rely on the MRT, enjoy the many free sights, and save your splurges for a memorable meal, a rooftop view or a special experience.",
