    fullDescription: "The honest answer to whether Singapore is expensive is: it depends what you spend on. Regularly ranked among the world's costliest cities, Singapore is genuinely pricey in several categories — hotel rooms command a premium, with even mid-range properties often costing more than their equivalents in many other cities; alcohol is heavily taxed, so a night of cocktails or beers adds up fast; car ownership is so restricted that this feeds into higher costs generally; and branded shopping is rarely a bargain. Yet in other crucial respects Singapore offers outstanding value, which is what surprises many first-time visitors. Its food, above all, is a revelation: the hawker centres that are the heart of local life serve some of the best-value great food anywhere on earth, with a full, delicious meal costing only S$5 to S$8, and even a Michelin-recognised hawker plate available for around S$6. Public transport is similarly cheap and superb, with MRT and bus fares of a dollar or two, and a great many of the city's headline attractions and experiences — its gardens, waterfront, light shows, temples, cultural neighbourhoods and beaches — are free to enjoy. So the key to managing costs is balance: you might splash out on a spectacular rooftop cocktail or a fine-dining dinner one evening, then eat brilliantly for a few dollars at a hawker centre the next, ride the efficient MRT rather than taxis, and fill your days with the abundant free sights. Handled this way, Singapore need not break the bank, and the money you do spend, particularly on food, tends to deliver exceptional quality. It is a city where budget and luxury sit side by side, and where the savviest travellers enjoy the best of both.",
